  "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-1)}^{\u2032}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-1){({e}^{-{x}^{2}})}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-1)(-2x){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}(1-({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2x(2-{x}^{2}){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2x({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ -2x(x-\\sqrt{2})(x+\\sqrt{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\sqrt{2},\\,0,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ $x=0,$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=0,\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$",
